private CSVFormat(final char delimiter, final Character quoteChar, final QuoteMode quoteMode,
                  final Character commentStart, final Character escape, final boolean ignoreSurroundingSpaces,
                  final boolean ignoreEmptyLines, final String recordSeparator, final String nullString,
                  final Object[] headerComments, final String[] header, final boolean skipHeaderRecord,
                  final boolean allowMissingColumnNames, final boolean ignoreHeaderCase, final boolean trim,
                  final boolean trailingDelimiter, final boolean autoFlush) {
    // Initialization code
    this.delimiter = delimiter;
    this.quoteCharacter = quoteChar;
    this.quoteMode = quoteMode;
    // Other properties assignment
    this.autoFlush = autoFlush;
    validate();
}
```

Based on the provided information, the issue might lie in how the quote character is handled in the `CSVFormat` configuration, especially when using `CSVFormat.EXCEL`. We should investigate how the quote character is set and used in both Apache Commons CSV and Microsoft Excel to identify any discrepancies that may cause the observed difference in behavior.

